Let $E$ be a normed additive commutative group, $f \colon [a, b] \to E$ a continuous function, and $f' \colon \mathbb{R} \to E$ a function. Suppose that:
1. $f$ has a right derivative $f'(x)$ at every point $x \in [a, b)$;
2. $\|f(a)\| \leq B(a)$ for some function $B \colon \mathbb{R} \to \mathbb{R}$ that is continuous on $[a, b]$ and has a right derivative $B'(x)$ at every $x \in [a, b)$;
3. For every $x \in [a, b)$, $\|f'(x)\| \leq B'(x)$.

Then $\|f(x)\| \leq B(x)$ for all $x \in [a, b]$.

